 "capsule": "Across this long span, the term 'Ceylon' designates the succession of Sinhalese kingdoms that governed most of the island after the Dambadeniya dynasty's recovery from Kalinga Magha's occupation: the capital moved from Dambadeniya to Yapahuwa (1273), Kurunegala, and Gampola (c.1341) before Alagakkonara's fortress at Sri Jayawardhanapura-Kotte became the seat of Parakramabahu VI, who reunified the whole island under Kotte rule from 1412 to 1467. Buddhism, Sinhala literature, and custody of the sacred Tooth Relic remained the unifying threads of royal legitimacy through these shifting capitals, even as Pandyan, Malay and Chinese naval interventions repeatedly disrupted the throne. The era named here closes in 1507, two years after a storm-driven Portuguese fleet under Lourenço de Almeida first reached Kotte in 1505, opening the age of European contact that would fracture the island's kingdoms within a generation.",
